    "available_at": "2026-11-23T21:53:46.786642+00:00",
    "body": "Stacks of copper cathode at Gunnison Copper's Johnson Camp mine in Arizona. Credit: Blair McBride\nPosted By: Blair McBride November 23, 2026\nSpeculative investors reduced their net bullish positions in copper futures by nearly 39% in the latest reporting week, according to BMO Capital Markets, as selling pressure intensified amid uncertainty over U.S. tariffs and weakening enthusiasm for artificial intelligence investments. It was the eighth straight weekly decline.\nNet bullish positions held by non-commercial traders in U.S. copper futures fell to 17,777 contracts as of Nov. 17, down from 28,904 a week earlier and at their lowest level since March 2025, BMO analysts Helen Amos and George Heppel said Monday, citing Commodity Futures Trading Commission data released Friday.\n“Our conversations here in China over Asia Copper Week indicate onshore expectations are for the U.S. to abandon tariff policy, a view further fuelled by reports that tens of [thousands of tonnes] of cathode in the U.S. have been booked for direct dispatch to China,” Amos and Heppel said Friday in a note from Shanghai.\nYangshan premium down\nChina’s Yangshan warrant premium, a measure of demand for imported copper, eased to $450 (C$646) per tonne Monday from a record $500 last week, Reuters reported. Another 11,225 tonnes of copper were earmarked for removal from London Metal Exchange warehouses in Asia on Friday, with a trader and warehouse manager telling Reuters the metal was destined for China.\nThe sell-off has intensified as investors close positions betting on higher copper prices and increase wagers on further declines, BMO said Friday. The analysts said the exercise of put options, which give holders the right to sell at a predetermined price, has amplified the correction. Speculators shed 7,268 long contracts and added 3,859 short contracts over the week, the commission’s figures show.\nThree-month copper on the London Metal Exchange traded at $12,193.50 per tonne as of 6:11 a.m. Toronto time Monday, up 0.3%, after briefly falling to $11,948, its lowest level since March 24, Reuters reported. Copper has lost almost 14% this month as investors question whether heavy spending on artificial intelligence will generate sufficient returns, despite tight physical supplies in China.\nCopper reverses course\nThe analysts’ assessment came Friday as traders prepared to ship an estimated 30,000 to 100,000 tonnes of refined copper from the U.S. West Coast to China, a move BMO had previously considered highly unlikely. The shipments reverse a flow of metal into American warehouses ahead of possible U.S. import tariffs.\nMeanwhile, Chilean producer Codelco, one of the world’s top copper producers, has offered Asian buyers annual contract premiums of $250 to $280 per tonne for 2027, compared with $350 for Chinese buyers this year, Reuters reported, citing people familiar with the discussions. China imported just over 140,000 tonnes of Chilean cathode in the first nine months of 2026, down 38% from a year earlier.\nPrice outlook\nBMO warned that downward pressure on copper prices could persist until the administration of President Donald Trump signals its tariff intentions or scrap and solvent extraction-electrowinning producers respond to market conditions.\nA July 2025 White House proclamation imposed a 50% tariff on certain semi-finished copper products and derivatives, while leaving refined copper exempt pending further review. The administration is considering duties of 15% on refined copper from Jan. 1, 2027, and 30% from Jan. 1, 2028.\nA signal from Washington could stop the slide. Otherwise, the red metal might have to fall far enough to curb scrap supply or higher-cost production from leaching, potentially to around $10,000 to $11,000 per tonne, Amos and Heppel said. BMO’s medium-term bullish view on copper is unchanged.\nPrint\nCOMMODITY: Copper\nREGION: United States\nRepublish this article",
